xnu-boot.img contains, after the caiman loader (MIT, this repository):

  hakios-kernel -- HackiOS's build of Apple's open-source XNU (xnu-8796.121.2, Darwin 22.5.0,
  DEVELOPMENT_ARM64_BCM2837), taken unmodified from
  https://github.com/oosexgang/hackios-xnu-kernel/blob/63fc2b4/prebuilt/hakios-kernel
  sha256 03e794e8e1d389a6abb9bcd2431eaccf9747271ff8c1234922225718c70441e9
  (matches that repository's prebuilt/SHA256SUMS).
  Licensed under the Apple Public Source License 2.0 (XNU, and HackiOS's kernel changes);
  the source is Apple's xnu-8796.121.2 plus that repository's xnu/ directory.
  The loader patches three instructions of it in RAM at boot (console -> loader_log), as
  HackiOS's own MediaTek loader does.

hello-boot.img contains the same, and as its RAM disk:

  hello -- HackiOS's raw-syscall first program (userland/src/hello.c, MIT, (c) the HakiOS
  developers), taken unmodified from
  https://github.com/oosexgang/hackios-xnu-kernel/blob/63fc2b4/prebuilt/userland/hello
  sha256 48a910940230506813aa0b75692e58d1c6698feccf7c6f7771c8790b6c97522d
  (matches that repository's prebuilt/SHA256SUMS). XNU's mockfs runs it as /sbin/launchd.

No file of iOS or of any Apple firmware is included. The device identity in the loader's
device tree is a placeholder (no Apple data).

The loader's XNU path (probe/xnu.c, probe/watcher.c, probe/handoff.S) follows HackiOS's
mtkloader (hakios-mt6768) and xnuloader (hackios-xnu-kernel), MIT, (c) the HakiOS developers.
